From Kirk@ntsol.com Thu, 29 Oct 1998 09:37:34 -0800
Message-ID: <D2D481C69863D11193DA00009296E5E3037DD2@titan.ntsol.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 1998 09:37:34 -0800
From: Kirk Erichsen <Kirk@ntsol.com>
Subject: RE: 66MHz DIMM support on RX? (was :Re:Here what I figured!)
To: 'AlphaNT Mail List' <AlphaNT@AlphaNT.com>
Reply-To: AlphaNT Mail List <AlphaNT@AlphaNT.com>

The memory used on all systems using the Pyxis chipset is, unfortunately,
limited to 66MHz operation.  The 100MHz memory used on these systems is
high-spec unbuffered 3.3 volt SDRAM (parity or ECC) DIMMS, but they still
only run on existing Alpha systems using Pyxis at 66MHz.  It's pretty common
to see memory speced to higher frequencies used on systems with slower
memory bus frequencies simply because it usually improves reliability.  Many
meatballs with Intel PII systems and the 440BX chipset are using 10ns 100MHz
memory maxed out at spec, which means there is very little room for error.
As a matter of academic interest, "100MHz" 10ns memory is best deployed at
83.5MHz bus speeds where 8ns SDRAM is the most suitable for 100MHz
applications, leaving a margin of error (and in the commodity DRAM markets
there is a TON of that).  If you want to run your memory bus at 166MHz, you
memory should spec out at 5ns. One interesting development is the new DDR
SDRAM/DDR ESDRAM standards which double the clock on the memory (say 200MHz
instead of 100MHz) by using double-edged triggering, a technique becoming
rather commonplace with the advent of the AGP port (66MHz real, 133MHz
effective) and even the backline cache and main memory buses of the 21264
(running at 333MHz).    10ns "100MHz" SDRAM used on existing LX, UX, SX
boards should work fine in RX boards providing they have the serial detect
SROM (the table for communicating memory capabilities and optimal timings to
the chipset/firmware).  This is pretty much the same gold-pin memory used on
the bulk of Intel 440BX/450NX chipset systems (use what is available).  

